-- title:
--   Torsion points of ℚ/ℤ have denominator any multiple of the order
-- statement:
--   Let $t$ be an element of `AddCircle (1 : ℚ)`, that is, of the quotient $\mathbb{Q}/\mathbb{Z}$ of the additive group of rationals by the subgroup generated by $1$. Let $n$ and $N$ be natural numbers with $N > 0$ and $n \mid N$, and suppose that $n \cdot t = 0$ in $\mathbb{Q}/\mathbb{Z}$. Then there is an integer $a$ such that $t$ is the image of the rational number $a/N$ under the quotient map $\mathbb{Q} \to \mathbb{Q}/\mathbb{Z}$. In other words, an $n$-torsion element of $\mathbb{Q}/\mathbb{Z}$ lies in the image of $\frac{1}{N}\mathbb{Z}$ for every positive multiple $N$ of $n$; the conclusion produces the integer numerator $a$ with the prescribed denominator $N$, not merely the minimal one.
--
--   This is the elementary normalisation lemma for torsion in $\mathbb{Q}/\mathbb{Z}$: an element killed by $n$ can be written with any chosen denominator divisible by $n$. It is used to put a torsion value of an invariant map, whose order divides the cardinality of a local decomposition group, into the form $a/N$ with $N$ that cardinality, in [`NumberField.LevelArith.exists_level_d_two_three_eq_of_sIdele_coboundary_of_smul_eq_of_dvd_natCard_decomp`](thm.html#NumberField.LevelArith.exists_level_d_two_three_eq_of_sIdele_coboundary_of_smul_eq_of_dvd_natCard_decomp).
